WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.928
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.993 --> 00:00:08.410
SPEAKER: This
quickstart shows you

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:10.510
how to use Cloud Build
to build a Docker image

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:13.210
and push the image to
Container Registry.

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:15.550
You will first build the
image using a Docker file

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:19.520
and then build the same image
using a configuration file.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.770
Before you begin, make sure
you have a Google Cloud

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:24.470
project selected and that you
have installed and initialized

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:26.970
the Cloud SDK.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:30.120
Begin by running gcloud auth
login in the command line

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.070
to authorize a gcloud
command line tool

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.230
to access your project.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:37.260
Use gcloud config set project
to connect the gcloud tool

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.550
with your project.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:42.120
Now we'll create a simple Hello
World example and a Docker

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.468
file.

00:00:43.468 --> 00:00:46.010
Cloud Build allows you to build
a Docker image using a Docker

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:46.730
file.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:49.580
We don't require a
separate build config file.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.530
First, we'll create a
script for our container

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:56.220
to execute called quickstart.sh.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.410
Next, we'll create a
file called Docker file.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:02.470
Finally, in the
command line, we'll

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:07.510
make sure that
quickstart.sh is executable.

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:09.760
In the directory where
the Docker file is,

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.650
use the gcloud
build submit command

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:14.530
with the tag flag set to the
project name and image name

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:16.300
you want to create.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.160
If you have not enabled
the Cloud Build API,

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.800
you will be prompted
to do so now.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.350
You've just built a Docker
image using a Docker file

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.840
and pushed the image
to Container Registry.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:32.440
Let's build the same image using
a cloudbuild.yaml config file.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.990
In the same directory that
contains quickstart.sh

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:39.260
and the Docker file, create
a file named cloudbuild.yaml.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:42.110
This build config instructs
Cloud Build to perform tasks

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:43.550
based on your specifications.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:47.980
After we've built
cloudbuild.yaml,

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.440
we'll use the gcloud
builds submit command

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:53.800
with the config cloudbuild.yaml
flag to build our image

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.333
and push it to
Container Registry.

00:01:56.333 --> 00:01:58.000
Once we've built the
image and pushed it

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.490
to Container Registry, we
can use the Google Cloud

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:02.200
Console to view
the build details

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.330
on the Cloud Build page.

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:06.520
Once there, you will
see the build history.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.020
By clicking on a
specific image, you

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:10.143
can see the details
of that build.

00:02:10.143 --> 00:02:11.560
Now you've built
a container image

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.460
and pushed it into a
Container Registry, where

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:15.835
it can be used with other
parts of the Google Cloud Build

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:17.980
process.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:21.630
[MUSIC PLAYING]

